revamped CRN
============

CRN subspecies concept
----------------------
CRN species can be partitioned into subspecies. Simulation are performed on the subspecies
level (so that for example the subspecies RNA_mutant can react different from RNA_wildtype).
In contrast, interaction with the CRN, such as setting up simulations and reporting 
results are by default done on the species level, unless explicitly asked for.

From the user point of view, CRN.species should represent partitions, whereas computations
are performed on CRN.subspecies. (this seems related to pandas MultiIndexes)

So, I need a mapping (injection) from a CRN species state to subspecies to perform any simulation
on the subspecies. I then need a projection back from subspecies to CRN species -- which is just
adding up subspecies concentrations.

I can hold species and subspecies concentrations in the same trajectory xarray, and update
species concentrations after each simulation:

>>> for species, subspecies in crn.species_definitions:
...    traj[traj.species==species] = traj[traj.species.isin(subspecies)].sum(axis='species')


Currently:

>>> traj = crn.integrate(initial_state)
>>> for conc in traj:
...    plt.plot(species.time, conc)

If I want this to iterate on the CRN species level, the return value of CRN.integrate
(traj) needs to provide its own __iter__ and should otherwise behave like the original
DataArray. A property traj.subspecies could return an alternative iterator:

>>> traj = crn.integrate(initial_state)
>>> for conc in traj.subspecies:
...    plt.plot(species.time, conc)

I need to learn how iteration is done in xarray and how it plays together with numpy views...


CRN instantaneous reactions
---------------------------
    A + B -> X; k=inf
    A + C -> Y; k

da/dt = -a*b - a*c
db/dt = -a*b
dc/dt = -a*c
dx/dt = a*b
dy/dt = a*c

for x = [ 1,    1,    3,   0,   0]
d/dt =  [-4,   -1,   -3,   1,   3]
        [-1, -.25, -.75, .25, .75]
res =   [ 0,  .75. 2.25, .25, .75]

CRN DSL
-------

    A contains imp1 with p=0.1 
               imp2 with q=0.3

    A -> X
    A [imp1] -> Y; k=inf
    A [imp2] -> Z; k=1



    probe contains impure

    probe + input -> intermediate + protector
    probe [impure] + input -> stagnation


taj.sel(species="probe")
taj.sel(species="probe_impure")
taj.sel(species="probe_rest")


    input contains mutation rest wildtype

    probe + input [mutation] -> intermediate_mu; k = 1e7
    probe + input -> intermediate_wt; k = 1e6

traj.sel(species="input")
traj.sel(species="input_mutation")
traj.sel(species="input_wildtype")




new Assay thoughts
==================

I need a good treatment of errors to support quantitative fits. There are multiple sources of
errors: (a) measurement errors, (b) repeat errors or intra-sample errors, (c) inter-sample errors
or sample errors for short.

Measurement errors are the measurement noise associated with the signal from a single well.
They superimpose the signal and I can assume them to occur generally on a faster time scale than
reactions

Repeat errors are differences between multiple measurements obtained from the same sample, usually
by distributing the sample into several wells.

Sample errors are differences between different samples that ought to have the same content.


Assay should accept URL or file handle

Create property Assay.contents to declare plate layout.
Assay.calibrate can then simply be called with a group or sample name.
Assay.fit will should work with the default contents/calibration


assay = Assay('path.xlsx')
assay.contents = xr.DataArray(
    [
        ..
    ],
    { 'content': [...],
      'species': [...]}
)

assay.calibrate(assay.plate.sel(sample="Sample X16"), assay.plate.sel(sample="Sample X2"))

model = crn.from_string("""
    A + B -> C
""")

fit = assay.fit(model, positive="Sample X23", negative="Sample X2")
model.params = fit.params
